      en: `<h2 style="border-left:4px solid #E63946;padding-left:12px;margin-top:32px;">Overview</h2>
<p>LED sensor lights are designed for long life and minimal maintenance, but regular care will significantly extend their lifespan and keep them performing at their best. A well-maintained fixture can last 50,000+ hours — twice as long as one that is neglected. This guide covers everything you need to know about routine maintenance and troubleshooting.</p>

<h2 style="border-left:4px solid #E63946;padding-left:12px;margin-top:32px;">Regular Cleaning</h2>
<p>Dust, dirt, and grime accumulate on the lens and sensor window over time, reducing light output and weakening detection sensitivity. Clean your fixtures every 3–6 months, or more frequently in dusty or coastal environments.</p>
<h3 style="margin-top:24px;color:#E63946;">Cleaning Steps</h3>
<ol style="line-height:2;">
<li><strong>Turn off power</strong> at the circuit breaker before cleaning. For solar models, cover the solar panel.</li>
<li><strong>Wipe the lens</strong> with a soft, lint-free microfiber cloth dampened with mild soap solution or glass cleaner.</li>
<li><strong>Clean the sensor window</strong> gently — do not scratch the plastic dome surface, as this affects detection accuracy.</li>
<li><strong>Wipe the housing</strong> with a damp cloth to remove surface dirt and bird droppings.</li>
<li><strong>Dry thoroughly</strong> with a clean cloth to prevent water spots.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Never use</strong> abrasive cleaners, scouring pads, or harsh chemicals — they can damage the lens coating and sensor window.</p>

<h2 style="border-left:4px solid #E63946;padding-left:12px;margin-top:32px;">Periodic Inspection Checklist</h2>
<p>Perform a thorough inspection every 6–12 months, or after severe weather (storms, hail, heavy snow):</p>
<ul style="line-height:2;">
<li><strong>Mounting hardware</strong> — Check that all screws and brackets are tight and not corroded</li>
<li><strong>Weather seals</strong> — Inspect gaskets and sealant for cracks or shrinkage; re-seal if needed</li>
<li><strong>Wiring connections</strong> — Look for frayed wires, loose connections, or signs of water damage</li>
<li><strong>LED array</strong> — Check for any dark spots or discolored LEDs (early failure signs)</li>
<li><strong>Sensor function</strong> — Walk-test detection range and response time</li>
<li><strong>Ground connection</strong> — Verify earth grounding is intact for safety</li>
<li><strong>Solar panel</strong> (if applicable) — Clean surface, check battery voltage, verify charge controller</li>
</ul>

<h2 style="border-left:4px solid #E63946;padding-left:12px;margin-top:32px;">Common Troubleshooting</h2>
<h3 style="margin-top:24px;color:#E63946;">Light Does Not Turn On</h3>
<p>First check the power source and circuit breaker. Test with a voltage meter at the fixture. If power is present, check the LED driver (power supply) — a failed driver is the most common cause of total failure. If the driver has visible swelling or burnt marks, it needs replacement.</p>
<h3 style="margin-top:24px;color:#E63946;">Sensor Not Detecting Motion</h3>
<p>Clean the sensor lens first — dirt buildup is the #1 cause of reduced sensitivity. Check the SENS (sensitivity) dial and ensure it is not set to minimum. Verify that nothing is physically blocking the sensor view. If the problem persists after cleaning and adjustment, the sensor module may need replacement.</p>
<h3 style="margin-top:24px;color:#E63946;">Flickering or Strobing</h3>
<p>Flickering usually indicates a loose connection or a failing LED driver. Turn off power and inspect all wire connections. If connections are solid, the driver is likely reaching end-of-life and should be replaced before it fails completely.</p>
<h3 style="margin-top:24px;color:#E63946;">Light Stays On Continuously</h3>
<p>Check that the TIME dial is not stuck at maximum. Clean the sensor lens — a dirty sensor can falsely trigger. Verify that the LUX (ambient light) setting is correct; if set too high, the light may stay on even during the day. If everything checks out, a stuck relay in the sensor electronics is likely.</p>
<h3 style="margin-top:24px;color:#E63946;">Water Inside the Fixture</h3>
<p>If you notice condensation or water inside the housing, turn off power immediately. Check and replace the gasket seals, re-apply silicone sealant around cable entries, and ensure the fixture is properly angled (lens facing slightly downward helps water drain). Dry the interior completely before restoring power.</p>

<h2 style="border-left:4px solid #E63946;padding-left:12px;margin-top:32px;">Replacing Components</h2>
<p>Quality LED sensor lights are designed with modular components that can be replaced individually:</p>
<ul style="line-height:2;">
<li><strong>LED Driver</strong> — The most frequently replaced part (typically 25,000–50,000 hour lifespan). Replace with the same wattage and output voltage.</li>
<li><strong>Sensor Module</strong> — Can be upgraded or replaced separately. Ensure the replacement matches the mounting pattern and voltage.</li>
<li><strong>LED Array</strong> — LED chips last 50,000+ hours, so replacement is uncommon. Check warranty before replacing.</li>
<li><strong>Gaskets & Seals</strong> — Replace every 2–3 years or whenever the fixture is opened for service.</li>
</ul>
<p>Always use manufacturer-approved replacement parts to maintain waterproof ratings and safety certifications.</p>

<h2 style="border-left:4px solid #E63946;padding-left:12px;margin-top:32px;">Tips to Extend Lifespan</h2>
<ul style="line-height:2;">
<li><strong>Keep it clean</strong> — Dirt on the lens traps heat, accelerating LED degradation. Regular cleaning also keeps the sensor working properly.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid over-voltage</strong> — Use surge protectors or voltage stabilizers where grid voltage fluctuates. Voltage spikes are a major cause of early driver failure.</li>
<li><strong>Proper ventilation</strong> — Never paint over or cover heat sink fins. LEDs run cooler = longer life.</li>
<li><strong>Install under eaves when possible</strong> — Protection from direct rain and sun extends both fixture and seal life.</li>
<li><strong>Use dimming where appropriate</strong> — Running LEDs at reduced current (e.g., 30% brightness for most of the night) dramatically extends lifespan.</li>
<li><strong>Replace gaskets proactively</strong> — Don't wait for water intrusion to replace seals; a 2-minute gasket replacement prevents costly repairs.</li>
<li><strong>Seasonal adjustment</strong> — Reduce sensitivity in summer when tree foliage sways in the wind; increase it in winter when branches are bare.</li>
</ul>

<h2 style="border-left:4px solid #E63946;padding-left:12px;margin-top:32px;">Conclusion</h2>
<p>Regular maintenance pays for itself many times over in extended fixture life and consistent performance. A simple twice-yearly cleaning and inspection routine takes only minutes per fixture but can add years of reliable service. When issues do arise, address them promptly — small problems like a loose seal or dirty lens, left unaddressed, can lead to complete fixture failure. With proper care, quality LED sensor lights can provide 10+ years of dependable service.</p>`,
